    contentEn: `<p>A private transfer from Pamplona to Zaragoza Airport (ZAZ) covers around 185 kilometres and takes roughly 1 hour and 50 minutes by car, running south from the city on the A-15 and AP-68 motorways directly to the terminal on the western edge of Zaragoza, without any need to route through Zaragoza city centre. Your driver collects you from your hotel, apartment, or other address in Pamplona at the time you choose, builds in appropriate time for check-in and security, and drops you at departures with your flight schedule already factored into the journey.</p>
<p>This route is designed for travellers departing from Pamplona — whether after the Fiestas de San Fermín, a quieter stay exploring the Casco Antiguo, or a trip through wider Navarre — who are flying out of Zaragoza Airport rather than a smaller regional airport, often because ZAZ offers more convenient flight connections. Because the airport sits on the western side of Zaragoza, a transfer coming down from Pamplona can reach the terminal directly on the motorway, bypassing the city centre entirely rather than adding time by cutting through it.</p>
<p>Confirming your flight's status on <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> in the days before departure lets you double-check timings, but the pickup itself is already built around your scheduled flight and adjusted automatically if that changes. Every detail of the roughly two-hour journey south — pickup address, price, and vehicle — is settled in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, so departure morning in Pamplona involves nothing more than stepping into the car when it arrives.</p>
<h2>About Pamplona</h2>
<p>Pamplona, the capital of Navarre, sits roughly 175 kilometres north of Zaragoza and traces its origins to a Roman-era settlement, later growing into the fortified, strategically important city whose walls and citadel still define much of its historic centre today. The Casco Antiguo, Pamplona's old town, remains a genuinely lived-in quarter of narrow streets, historic squares, and centuries-old buildings rather than a district preserved purely for visitors.</p>
<p>The city's global reputation rests largely on the Fiestas de San Fermín, held every year from 6 to 14 July, and in particular on the encierro, the Running of the Bulls, which takes place each morning from 7 to 14 July at exactly 8:00am. Participants run alongside released bulls along a barricaded route through the old town to the bullring, an event watched and reported on around the world, and one that transforms Pamplona into one of the busiest, most crowded cities in Spain for those nine days each summer.</p>
<p>Outside the festival, Pamplona settles into a considerably calmer rhythm, and its architectural and historical interest stands on its own. The Gothic cathedral, the elegant Plaza del Castillo at the heart of the old town, and the well-preserved city walls and star-shaped citadel all reflect Pamplona's long history as a strategic stronghold on the Spanish side of the Pyrenees, guarding routes between Spain and France for centuries.</p>
<p>Navarre's broader landscape, from the greener countryside toward the Pyrenees and the Basque Country in the north to the drier plains further south, gives Pamplona a transitional character that shows up throughout the region — in its cuisine, its architecture, and the pace of daily life outside the intensity of festival week.</p>
<h3>Who This Transfer Suits</h3>
<p>Travellers concluding a stay in Pamplona, whether for San Fermín, a cultural city break, or business in Navarre, and flying out of Zaragoza Airport are the clearest fit for this route. Because the transfer runs directly from Pamplona to the terminal without a detour through Zaragoza city, it particularly suits departures on a fixed schedule where a predictable, direct journey matters more than any stop along the way — especially important during the festival period, when local roads and transport around Pamplona itself are under considerable strain.</p>
<h3>The Casco Antiguo and City Walls</h3>
<p>Pamplona's city walls, built up over centuries to defend a location of real strategic importance near the Pyrenean passes into France, remain remarkably well preserved compared with the fortifications of many other Spanish cities, and the star-shaped citadel adds a further layer of military architecture to explore on foot. Walking sections of the walls gives a clear sense of Pamplona's historic role as a border stronghold rather than simply a regional capital.</p>
<p>Within the walls, the Casco Antiguo's narrow streets converge on Plaza del Castillo, a large, arcaded square that has served for centuries as the social heart of the city, surrounded by cafés and historic buildings that give a strong sense of everyday Pamplona life outside the festival calendar.</p>
<h3>Departing After San Fermín</h3>
<p>Travellers flying out shortly after the Fiestas de San Fermín conclude should plan for a city still settling back into its normal rhythm, with road access and traffic patterns around the old town gradually returning to usual after nine days of intense celebration. Booking a private transfer in advance for this period is particularly worthwhile, since demand for transport around Pamplona in the days immediately following the festival remains high as visitors from around the world head home at roughly the same time.</p>
<h3>Navarre Beyond Pamplona</h3>
<p>Travellers departing from Pamplona often arrive there after exploring more of Navarre than the capital alone, and the region rewards that wider itinerary. Olite, roughly 40 kilometres south of Pamplona, is known for its medieval royal castle, one of the most striking examples of Gothic civil architecture in Spain, while Roncesvalles, in the Pyrenean foothills to the north, marks a historic gateway on the Camino de Santiago, the pilgrimage route that has drawn walkers into Navarre for centuries.</p>
<p>For travellers who have spent time in Navarre's wine country, its mountain valleys, or its smaller medieval towns before returning to Pamplona for their flight, a private transfer to Zaragoza Airport offers a single, comfortable final leg after what is often a multi-stop trip, rather than requiring a change of vehicle or an extra night arranged purely around transport logistics.</p>
<h2>Getting There to Zaragoza Airport</h2>
<p>The drive from Pamplona to Zaragoza Airport runs primarily on the A-15 motorway before joining the AP-68 for the remainder of the journey south, a well-maintained route covering the roughly 185 kilometres in about 1 hour and 50 minutes under normal conditions. Because the airport sits west of Zaragoza city, the journey bypasses the city centre entirely, keeping the drive time predictable regardless of traffic inside Zaragoza itself.</p>
<p>Your driver builds in buffer time for check-in and security ahead of your flight, so rather than watching the clock on the road, you can treat the drive from Pamplona as a calculated, settled part of your travel day.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether you're departing after San Fermín or the tail end of a quieter trip through Navarre, booking your transfer to the airport in advance keeps departure day simple. See our <a href="/airport-transfers/">airport transfers</a> page for more on how our pickups and drop-offs work, our <a href="/zaragoza-airport-transfer/">Zaragoza airport transfer</a> page for details on ZAZ itself, and our <a href="/corporate-travel/">corporate travel</a> service if you're travelling to or from Navarre for business.</p>`,
